    <div class="space-y-10">
      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Steel Weight Calculator – Calculate Steel Weight in KG
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          A <strong>steel weight calculator</strong> is an indispensable civil engineering, fabrication, and material estimation tool designed to calculate the precise weight of reinforcing rebar, steel plates, solid round and square bars, hollow sections (SHS/RHS), steel pipes, structural profiles (I-beams, channels, angles), and stainless steel grades in <strong>kilograms (kg)</strong> and <strong>metric tonnes (MT)</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          Accurately estimating steel weight is vital for construction cost budgeting, freight logistics, structural engineering analysis, and procurement verification from steel rolling mills.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          What Is a Steel Weight Calculator?
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          A <strong>steel weight calculator</strong> computes the gravitational mass of steel products by calculating their geometric volume and multiplying by the material's specific density:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 font-mono space-y-1">
          <div class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">Fundamental Steel Weight Formula:</div>
          <div>Weight (kg) = Volume (m³) × Material Density (kg/m³)</div>
          <div class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">For prismatic shapes: Weight = Cross-Sectional Area (m²) × Length (m) × Density (kg/m³)</div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          How to Use This Steel Weight Calculator
        </h2>
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <li><strong>Select Steel Type:</strong> Choose between TMT Rebar, Round Bar, Plate, Pipe, Square/Rectangular Hollow Section, Angle, Channel, Beam, or Stainless Steel.</li>
          <li><strong>Select Material Grade:</strong> Choose Mild Steel (7,850 kg/m³), Stainless Steel 304 (7,930 kg/m³), Stainless Steel 316 (8,000 kg/m³), or Cast Iron.</li>
          <li><strong>Enter Dimensions:</strong> Input the required cross-sectional dimensions (diameter, width, height, or wall thickness in millimeters).</li>
          <li><strong>Enter Length & Quantity:</strong> Specify the linear length in meters and the total number of pieces.</li>
          <li><strong>Click Calculate Weight:</strong> Press the blue <strong>"Calculate Weight"</strong> button to reveal the weight in kilograms and metric tonnes.</li>
          <li><strong>Use Clear:</strong> Click <strong>"Clear (✕)"</strong> anytime to reset all inputs and start a new calculation.</li>
          <li><strong>Download PDF:</strong> Save or print an estimate sheet with full worked formulas.</li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Rebar Weight Calculator & The D²/162 Formula
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
          For cylindrical deformed rebar (TMT bars), unit weight per linear meter is computed using the time-tested civil engineering formula:
        </p>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-4 text-xs sm:text-sm dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900 font-mono space-y-1">
          <div class="font-bold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400">Rebar Unit Weight Formula:</div>
          <div>Weight per Meter (kg/m) = D² ÷ 162.28  ≈  D² ÷ 162</div>
          <div class="text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">Where D is the nominal rebar diameter in millimeters (mm).</div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">
          <strong>Derivation:</strong> The density of mild structural steel is 7,850 kg/m³. The volume of 1 meter of a cylinder of diameter D mm is (π × D² ÷ 4) × 10⁻⁶ × 1 m. Multiplying by 7,850 kg/m³ gives:
          <code>Weight = (3.14159265 × 7850 ÷ 4,000,000) × D² = D² ÷ 162.28 kg/m</code>.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <li><strong>8 mm Rebar:</strong> 8² ÷ 162 = 0.395 kg/m (12m bar = 4.74 kg)</li>
          <li><strong>10 mm Rebar:</strong> 10² ÷ 162 = 0.617 kg/m (12m bar = 7.41 kg)</li>
          <li><strong>12 mm Rebar:</strong> 12² ÷ 162 = 0.889 kg/m (12m bar = 10.67 kg)</li>
          <li><strong>16 mm Rebar:</strong> 16² ÷ 162 = 1.580 kg/m (12m bar = 18.96 kg)</li>
          <li><strong>20 mm Rebar:</strong> 20² ÷ 162 = 2.469 kg/m (12m bar = 29.63 kg)</li>
          <li><strong>25 mm Rebar:</strong> 25² ÷ 162 = 3.858 kg/m (12m bar = 46.30 kg)</li>
          <li><strong>32 mm Rebar:</strong> 32² ÷ 162 = 6.313 kg/m (12m bar = 75.76 kg)</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Structural Steel Profile Weight Formulas
        </h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Round Steel Bar</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"><code>Weight = π × (D/2)² × Length × Density ÷ 1,000,000</code></p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Steel Plate / Sheet</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"><code>Weight = Width (m) × Length (m) × Thickness (mm) × 7.85</code></p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Steel Pipe / Round Tube</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"><code>Weight = (OD - Thickness) × Thickness × 0.02466 × Length (m)</code></p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Square Hollow Section (SHS)</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400"><code>Weight = (W² - (W - 2T)²) × Length × Density ÷ 1,000,000</code></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-3">
        <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-black text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
          Common Mistakes When Estimating Steel Weight
        </h2>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-3 sm:grid-cols-2 text-xs sm:text-sm">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Ignoring Rolling Margins</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Rolling tolerance ranges from ±2% to ±7% under IS 1786 / ASTM A615. Real rebar weights can fluctuate around theoretical values.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Neglecting Lap Lengths</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Structural splices, development lap lengths (40d to 50d), and hook bends add 5% to 10% to gross linear rebar procurement.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Using Wrong Stainless Steel Density</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">Stainless steel 304 (7,930 kg/m³) and 316 (8,000 kg/m³) are denser than mild carbon steel (7,850 kg/m³).</p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-slate-200 p-3.5 dark:border-slate-800">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Confusing Inner vs Outer Diameters</h3>
            <p class="mt-1 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">For hollow pipes, always ensure outer diameter (OD) and nominal wall thickness are entered accurately.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

